==================================== INPUT ====================================
// Test: `@UNSTABLE_key` on an INTERFACE, which the directive declares as a valid location
// (`repeatable on OBJECT | INTERFACE`) and which no fixture covered.
//
// `key_granted_fields` has a separate `Type::Interface` arm from its
// `Type::Object` one, and every existing `@UNSTABLE_key` fixture uses `extend type`. The
// interface arm's only signal was the `ads_frameworks_lab_intern` www build,
// which grants `extend interface Actor @UNSTABLE_key(fields: "name")`.
//
// The question this pins is WHERE the grant has to live, and the answer is
// narrower than the directive's `on OBJECT | INTERFACE` suggests. The grant is
// looked up on the SELECTED FIELD's resolved parent type, so an interface grant
// applies only where the selection's parent type IS that interface. All three
// cases below are here because the interesting part is that they differ:
//
//   - `display_name` selected directly on `User` is REJECTED. Relay resolves the
//     field against `User`, which carries no grant. The grant on `Actor` is not
//     inherited by implementors.
//   - the SAME field under `... on Actor` is ACCEPTED, because there the parent
//     type is the interface. This is what makes the rejection above a
//     lookup-location limitation rather than the interface arm being dead.
//   - `handle` is declared on `User` and granted nowhere, so it is rejected in
//     both worlds — the control that stops this fixture from passing against an
//     implementation that treated any reachable `@UNSTABLE_key` as blanket permission.
//
// The middle case is why `ads_frameworks_lab_intern` works with
// `extend interface Actor @UNSTABLE_key(fields: "name")`: its fragments select through
// the interface. An author who annotates an interface and then selects the field
// on a concrete type gets a compile error pointing at the field, with a grant
// sitting right there on the interface that does not apply to it. Whether that
// SHOULD be inherited is an open design question, deliberately not decided here;
// this fixture pins what the code does today so the answer is a visible change.
//- FooQuery.js
graphql`query FooQuery @exec_time_resolvers {
  me {
    greeting
  }
}`

//- User_greeting.js
/**
 * @relayField User.greeting: RelayResolverValue
 * @rootFragment UserGreetingFragment
 */
graphql`fragment UserGreetingFragment on User {
  id
  display_name
  ... on Actor {
    display_name
  }
  handle
}`

//- relay.config.json
{
  "language": "flow",
  "jsModuleFormat": "haste",
  "schema": "schema.graphql",
  "schemaExtensions": ["schema-extensions"],
  "featureFlags": {
    "enable_exec_time_resolvers_directive": true
  }
}

//- schema.graphql
type Query {
  me: User
}

interface Actor {
  display_name: String
}

type User implements Actor {
  id: ID!
  display_name: String
  handle: String
}

//- schema-extensions/extension.graphql
extend interface Actor @UNSTABLE_key(fields: "display_name")
==================================== OUTPUT ===================================
